WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.321 --> 00:00:07.076
Hi! In this video, I'll show you how to set up your accounts in

2
00:00:07.096 --> 00:00:08.594
Portfolio Tracker.

3
00:00:09.229 --> 00:00:12.895
We'll create two regular portfolios, and turn on Owner, so you

4
00:00:12.915 --> 00:00:14.234
can see them together.

5
00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:18.214
Then we'll import a Fidelity positions file, and a transactions

6
00:00:18.234 --> 00:00:22.449
file. At the end, you'll see how a ticker that's in both accounts

7
00:00:22.469 --> 00:00:24.298
adds up in the Owner view.

8
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.992
Everything starts on the Portfolios page.

9
00:00:31.037 --> 00:00:34.512
Open the Admin menu, and choose Portfolios.

10
00:00:34.558 --> 00:00:36.680
This is where all your accounts live.

11
00:00:37.318 --> 00:00:41.342
Right now the list is empty, so let's add our first account.

12
00:00:41.387 --> 00:00:44.390
Click New Portfolio, and give it a name.

13
00:00:44.448 --> 00:00:47.184
I'll call this one Fidelity Income Demo.

14
00:00:48.986 --> 00:00:51.747
So what's a regular portfolio?

15
00:00:51.792 --> 00:00:53.928
It's one real account at your broker.

16
00:00:53.986 --> 00:00:57.729
It keeps its own positions, its own cash, and its own history.

17
00:00:58.244 --> 00:01:01.131
Next, set the Broker Source to Fidelity.

18
00:01:01.151 --> 00:01:05.622
This one matters. It tells Portfolio Tracker which broker's files

19
00:01:05.642 --> 00:01:07.152
this account will accept.

20
00:01:07.198 --> 00:01:08.785
It saves right away.

21
00:01:09.329 --> 00:01:12.868
Account Type stays on User-owned, because this is a real account

22
00:01:12.888 --> 00:01:13.939
that I own.

23
00:01:14.458 --> 00:01:16.811
I'll add a second account the same way.

24
00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:20.612
New Portfolio, Fidelity Activity Demo, and OK.

25
00:01:20.644 --> 00:01:23.622
Then I'll set its Broker Source to Fidelity, too.

26
00:01:23.654 --> 00:01:25.943
Now we have two regular accounts.

27
00:01:26.975 --> 00:01:28.856
Now, let's talk about Owner.

28
00:01:28.901 --> 00:01:32.670
If you ever forget what it does, this help line up here explains

29
00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:33.307
it.

30
00:01:33.836 --> 00:01:35.334
Here's the short version.

31
00:01:35.405 --> 00:01:38.905
Owner is optional. It's not a brokerage account, and you never

32
00:01:38.925 --> 00:01:40.410
import files into it.

33
00:01:40.481 --> 00:01:42.451
Think of it as your personal rollup.

34
00:01:42.496 --> 00:01:46.086
It adds up the accounts you choose, and shows them as one.

35
00:01:46.633 --> 00:01:49.164
To turn it on, click Create Owner.

36
00:01:49.197 --> 00:01:52.952
You'll be asked to confirm, with a reminder that Owner is a rollup,

37
00:01:52.972 --> 00:01:54.572
not a brokerage account.

38
00:01:54.784 --> 00:01:55.950
I'll click OK.

39
00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:58.754
There it is, at the top of the list.

40
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.211
It's empty for now, because we haven't picked any accounts yet.

41
00:02:02.244 --> 00:02:05.374
And notice the selector up top switched over to Owner.

42
00:02:05.904 --> 00:02:09.915
To put accounts into Owner, just tick the Owner box on each row.

43
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.035
I'll check Fidelity Income Demo, and Fidelity Activity Demo.

44
00:02:14.526 --> 00:02:17.287
That's it. Owner now combines them.

45
00:02:17.345 --> 00:02:21.228
And see how its Broker Source changed to Rollup, no broker?

46
00:02:21.248 --> 00:02:23.843
Each account still stands on its own, though.

47
00:02:23.876 --> 00:02:27.529
Owner only adds them up for viewing, so nothing gets moved or

48
00:02:27.549 --> 00:02:28.447
copied.

49
00:02:28.983 --> 00:02:32.356
Down here, there's a similar tool called Aggregates.

50
00:02:32.402 --> 00:02:35.035
Click Add Aggregate, and give it a name.

51
00:02:35.093 --> 00:02:37.420
I'll use Demo Fidelity Aggregate.

52
00:02:39.347 --> 00:02:41.571
Then tick the accounts you want in it.

53
00:02:41.796 --> 00:02:43.816
I'll pick both Fidelity accounts.

54
00:02:44.372 --> 00:02:46.571
So how is this different from Owner?

55
00:02:46.591 --> 00:02:50.092
An aggregate is a read-only group that you name yourself.

56
00:02:50.112 --> 00:02:53.548
You can make as many as you like, and one account can be in more

57
00:02:53.568 --> 00:02:57.247
than one. Owner is the one main rollup of what you own.

58
00:02:57.293 --> 00:03:00.002
Aggregates are for any other grouping you want.

59
00:03:00.515 --> 00:03:02.791
Now let's bring in some data.

60
00:03:02.837 --> 00:03:07.217
Imports always go into a regular account, never into Owner.

61
00:03:07.276 --> 00:03:11.031
So first, pick the account in the selector at the top.

62
00:03:11.089 --> 00:03:13.455
I'll choose Fidelity Income Demo.

63
00:03:14.738 --> 00:03:17.001
Then open Admin, and Import.

64
00:03:17.046 --> 00:03:20.508
See how it says, Importing into Fidelity Income Demo?

65
00:03:20.554 --> 00:03:23.595
That's your check that the file lands in the right place.

66
00:03:24.078 --> 00:03:26.265
Start with Step 1, Positions.

67
00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:29.810
A positions file is a snapshot of what you own today.

68
00:03:29.907 --> 00:03:33.165
Fidelity's already picked for us, because of the Broker Source

69
00:03:33.185 --> 00:03:34.428
we set earlier.

70
00:03:34.933 --> 00:03:38.918
Now click the drop box, choose the Fidelity positions file, and

71
00:03:38.938 --> 00:03:40.040
click Preview.

72
00:03:40.568 --> 00:03:42.385
Always preview first.

73
00:03:42.418 --> 00:03:45.956
It shows the account named in the file, and what it found.

74
00:03:46.066 --> 00:03:49.222
Here we've got eight shares of VTI, a hundred and fifty shares

75
00:03:49.242 --> 00:03:53.137
of JEPQ, and twelve hundred and fifty dollars in cash.

76
00:03:53.652 --> 00:03:56.311
That looks right, so I'll click Import.

77
00:03:56.356 --> 00:03:58.824
And it brought in both positions.

78
00:03:59.359 --> 00:04:01.138
Now for the second account.

79
00:04:01.209 --> 00:04:04.913
I'll switch the selector to Fidelity Activity Demo, and choose

80
00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:07.107
Step 2, Transactions.

81
00:04:07.127 --> 00:04:09.633
A transactions file is your history.

82
00:04:09.691 --> 00:04:13.294
Buys, sells, dividends, and reinvestments.

83
00:04:13.801 --> 00:04:17.366
You'll see a warning when there's no positions snapshot yet.

84
00:04:17.398 --> 00:04:20.019
Normally, you'd import positions first.

85
00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:23.462
But this sample file is the full history for the account, so I'll

86
00:04:23.482 --> 00:04:25.452
tick the box and keep going.

87
00:04:25.975 --> 00:04:29.909
Pick the transactions file, and preview it, just like before.

88
00:04:31.201 --> 00:04:35.836
It found a buy of twelve shares of VTI, one VTI dividend, and

89
00:04:35.856 --> 00:04:38.425
one dividend reinvestment into JEPI.

90
00:04:38.445 --> 00:04:40.607
Looks good. Import it.

91
00:04:41.119 --> 00:04:45.258
Last, go to Step 3, and click Refresh Prices and Divs.

92
00:04:45.316 --> 00:04:48.791
After importing, it's a good habit to refresh, so Owner picks

93
00:04:48.811 --> 00:04:51.686
up the new holdings, along with current prices.

94
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.800
Now for the payoff.

95
00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:58.073
Switch the selector to Owner, and open Portfolio, then Holdings.

96
00:04:58.131 --> 00:05:01.543
This page lists every holding in whatever you've selected.

97
00:05:01.601 --> 00:05:03.443
Let's scroll down to the table.

98
00:05:04.207 --> 00:05:07.695
Look at VTI. It shows twenty shares.

99
00:05:07.727 --> 00:05:11.534
That's the eight shares from Fidelity Income Demo, plus the twelve

100
00:05:11.554 --> 00:05:13.575
from Fidelity Activity Demo.

101
00:05:13.608 --> 00:05:17.286
When the same ticker is in more than one account, Owner adds the

102
00:05:17.306 --> 00:05:19.493
shares together into one row.

103
00:05:20.018 --> 00:05:23.965
Tickers that are only in one account, like JEPQ and JEPI, keep

104
00:05:23.985 --> 00:05:25.483
their own share counts.

105
00:05:25.999 --> 00:05:29.398
And if I flip back to each account, you'll see the original numbers

106
00:05:29.418 --> 00:05:33.416
haven't changed. Fidelity Income Demo still has its eight shares

107
00:05:33.436 --> 00:05:34.602
of VTI.

108
00:05:35.149 --> 00:05:39.402
And Fidelity Activity Demo has its twelve shares of VTI, plus

109
00:05:39.422 --> 00:05:41.851
that small reinvestment into JEPI.

110
00:05:41.896 --> 00:05:43.917
Owner simply adds these up for you.

111
00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:47.644
The aggregate we made shows the same combined view.

112
00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:51.968
It's worked out live from its members, so nothing is stored twice.

113
00:05:53.287 --> 00:05:55.219
So, that's the setup.

114
00:05:55.251 --> 00:05:59.262
Make one regular portfolio for each real account, and import into

115
00:05:59.282 --> 00:06:03.191
that account. Turn on Owner when you want everything in one view,

116
00:06:03.262 --> 00:06:06.010
and use aggregates for any other groupings.

117
00:06:06.068 --> 00:06:07.362
Thanks for watching!

118
00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:10.887
For informational purposes only.

119
00:06:10.933 --> 00:06:13.000
Not financial advice.
